<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import WatchSourceDemo from "$ui/demos/watch/WatchSourceDemo.abide"
  import WatchSourcesDemo from "$ui/demos/watch/WatchSourcesDemo.abide"
  import WatchThunkDemo from "$ui/demos/watch/WatchThunkDemo.abide"
  import WatchTeardownDemo from "$ui/demos/watch/WatchTeardownDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1><code>watch</code> — the effect</h1>
  <p class="lead">The three primitives hold values; <code>watch</code> is how a value reaches something outside the graph. It is not a fourth primitive — it is the one escape hatch, and everything reactive in a rendered template is built on it. Like <a href="/state"><code>state</code></a>, <a href="/memo"><code>memo</code></a>, and <a href="/channel"><code>channel</code></a> it ships from <code>abide/shared/*</code>, so it is <strong>isomorphic</strong>: a <code>watch</code> in a plain server module fires on the server, with no client involved.</p>
  <Demo id="watch-thunk" title="<code>watch(thunk)</code> — auto-tracked effect" client="src/ui/demos/watch/WatchThunkDemo.abide">
    <WatchThunkDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Pass one function. It runs immediately and again whenever anything it read changes — nothing declares the dependency, the read <em>is</em> the declaration. The effect writes a cell it never reads, which is what keeps values flowing one way: in through the reads, out through the effect.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="watch-source" title="<code>watch(source, handler)</code> — change-only effect" client="src/ui/demos/watch/WatchSourceDemo.abide">
    <WatchSourceDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Pass a source thunk and two things change: the handler runs on change only — not on the initial read — and the dependency set is exactly what the thunk reads, whatever the handler touches.</p>
    <p>The source is <em>always</em> a thunk (ADR 0025), mirroring <code>memo(source, transform)</code>: same signature slot, same rule, second argument untracked in both.</p>
    <p class="note">Same inferred-or-declared split as <a href="/memo"><code>memo</code></a>; the two differ only in intent — a memo produces a value to be pulled, a watch performs an effect. That is why there is no separate "effect" primitive.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="watch-sources" title="<code>watch(() =&gt; ({'{'} a, b {'}'}), handler)</code> — several declared inputs" client="src/ui/demos/watch/WatchSourcesDemo.abide">
    <WatchSourcesDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Several inputs are just what the thunk returns, exactly as in <code>memo</code>. Every read inside it is tracked, and the <code>next</code>/<code>previous</code> the handler already receives carry it — so a handler reads what changed by name, never by position.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="watch-teardown" title="The returned teardown — re-run and unmount" client="src/ui/demos/watch/WatchTeardownTicker.abide">
    <WatchTeardownDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>An effect that takes something — a timer, a listener, a subscription — has to give it back. Either form may return a function, and that teardown runs at the two moments the resource must go: before every re-run, and when the effect is disposed. A component owns the effects its <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> creates, so disposal is that component going away — unmount in the browser, end of the request during SSR.</p>
    <p>Changing the interval re-runs the effect, and the log shows <code>stop</code> landing before the next <code>start</code>. Unmounting the ticker fires it one last time. The same already happened on the server: this page's SSR ran the effect and cleared its interval when the request finished.</p>
    <p>Holding one directly works the same way — <code>watch</code> returns a disposer, and calling it runs the teardown and detaches every read.</p>
    <p class="note">This is why the grammar has no <code>onMount</code>/<code>onDestroy</code>: the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> body is setup and the returned teardown is cleanup, so the pair stays in one place. It is also why this demo holds a real timer with no is-this-the-browser branch. <code>bind:element={'{'}fn{'}'}</code> takes the same shape for a node.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
